---
import SourcePanel from '../components/SourcePanel.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ASSETS } from '../data/assets';
import { SOURCES } from '../data/sources';

const title = 'Grave Seasons Pre-Launch Investigation Checklist';
const description =
  'A source-tracked Grave Seasons pre-launch checklist covering the one-year investigation, killer scenarios, Ashenridge, romance, and demo status.';
const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Pre-Launch Investigation Checklist', url: '/pre-launch-investigation-checklist/' },
];
const sources = [SOURCES.steam, SOURCES.steamDevlog, SOURCES.blumhouse, SOURCES.blumhouseMedia];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} image={ASSETS.townNight.src}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p class="section-kicker">Pre-launch planning - official sources</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-black leading-tight text-bone md:text-5xl">Grave Seasons Pre-Launch Investigation Checklist</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-fog">
      If you are searching for a Grave Seasons walkthrough before release, the useful answer is a
      preparation checklist rather than a fabricated route. Official Steam and Blumhouse material
      confirms the investigation frame, but not the final killer solution, quest order, or crop values.
    </p>

    <figure class="mt-8">
      <img src={ASSETS.townNight.src} alt={ASSETS.townNight.alt} width={ASSETS.townNight.width} height={ASSETS.townNight.height} class="rounded-md border border-white/10" loading="eager" />
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-fog">{ASSETS.townNight.credit}</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose-page mt-8">
      <h2>What the investigation is confirmed to involve</h2>
      <p>
        Steam describes Grave Seasons as a farming simulation where a supernatural serial killer is
        active in Ashenridge. The player has <strong>one year</strong> to protect potential victims and
        uncover the murders. The store page also lists more than <strong>30 residents</strong>, more than
        <strong>70 interiors</strong>, and <strong>seven major locations</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>Build a resident and location checklist</h2>
      <p>
        Before launch, record every official resident name, relationship clue, and location caption
        separately. The town is large enough that a useful live guide will need to connect residents,
        interiors, the farm, forest, coast, and the Local and Commercial districts. Do not turn a
        screenshot appearance into a confirmed quest or schedule.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Track the resident name and the source where it is officially shown.</li>
        <li>Keep location names separate from unconfirmed room or quest labels.</li>
        <li>After launch, add the in-game day, time, trigger, and outcome for every tested clue.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Expect replay differences, not one universal killer route</h2>
      <p>
        The Steam description says each playthrough selects a supernatural killer from a candidate
        pool, with killer-specific reasoning, kill styles, and targets. That means a single “best
        walkthrough” written before release would be misleading. The launch guide should branch by
        the killer scenario actually observed in the save, then label any missable result with a
        tested day and version.
      </p>

      <h2>Romance is part of the risk calculation</h2>
      <p>
        Steam lists <strong>12 potential romanceable folks</strong> and warns that a few may also be
        the killer. Treat romance pages as relationship tracking, not proof of a safe route. The
        practical launch checklist is to record the choice, resident, date, relationship result, and
        whether the playthrough changes the candidate pool.
      </p>

      <h2>Use the day-and-night cycle as a planning constraint</h2>
      <p>
        The official developer material describes a 24-hour cycle and a world that can change. Until
        the game is playable, the safe advice is to plan short evidence-based loops: farm or forage,
        review clues, speak with residents, and leave time for investigation. No exact stamina,
        shop-hour, crop-growth, or murder-timer number has been published here.
      </p>

      <h2>What is and is not available before launch</h2>
      <p>
        Perfect Garbage announced that the planned June 15 demo was cancelled when the full game was
        moved to <strong>Fall 2026</strong>. Steam currently shows the year only. There is no legitimate
        pre-release download to recommend, so wishlisting the official store page is the reliable way
        to receive a launch notification.
      </p>
      <p>
        The final release is officially planned for PC, Xbox Series X|S, PlayStation 5, and Nintendo
        Switch, with Day 1 Xbox Game Pass support announced. A specific day has not been published;
        this page will be updated when an official store or developer announcement narrows the window.
      </p>

      <h2>Launch-day evidence sheet</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Write down the build version and the in-game date before testing.</li>
        <li>Map the clue, resident, location, and time without assuming an outcome.</li>
        <li>Separate confirmed triggers from observations that happened only once.</li>
        <li>Repeat important choices in a second run before calling them universal.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <SourcePanel sources={sources} />
  </article>
</Layout>
